    "section_summary": {
      "type": "string",
      "extract_value": "This section (3.1 Data screening) reports missing data rates for participant height (0.5%), weight (8.2%), and age (8.0%); removal of improbable BMI values (<12 or >50 kg/m2; 2% of the dataset); Little's MCAR test result (\u03c72(20) = 43.422, p = .784); and that expectation-maximisation was used to replace missing values. It also notes no unduly influential univariate or multivariate outliers were found. No information about sample size, country, demographics, survey method, measures (BAS/BAS-2), mean age, mean BMI, or correlations is provided in this section.",
      "evidence_source": "Full provided text: \"3.1. Data screening\\nThere were missing data only for participant height (0.5%), weight (8.2%), and age (8.0%). We also removed improbable BMI values (<12 or >50 kg/m2; 2% of the total data set) and treated these as missing data. Little's (1988) MCAR analysis revealed that these data were missing completely at random, 2(20) = 43.422, p = .784. Therefore, we replaced missing values using expectation-maximisation. Further data screening did not reveal any unduly influential univariate or multivariate outliers.\"",
